  "body": "**What happens**\n\n`ff()` in `assets/posts/haas.cps` rounds an mm feed to a whole number of mm/min. For example, 12.4 mm/min posts as `F12.`. An inch feed keeps two decimals. #3681 (PR #3689) added the decimal point and did not change the precision. A positive feed that rounds to zero keeps its fraction since #3681. A feed of 1 mm/min or more is still rounded.\n\n**Why it matters**\n\nThe error is under 0.5 mm/min. At a low feed it can be a large part of the value. Examples are a slow finish pass, a G84 tap feed that must equal pitch x rpm, and a probe touch feed. A tap feed that is not exactly pitch x rpm can damage the thread or the tap.\n\n**What should happen**\n\nDecide the mm feed precision, for example one or three decimals, as the length words use. Then regenerate the Haas goldens. Check the tap feed first: it must not lose the pitch x rpm value.\n\n**Done means**\n\n- A test posts a fractional mm feed (for example 12.4 mm/min) and a tap feed, and checks the F word.\n- A decision file records the precision and its source.\n\nFound during #3681 (PR #3689).\n",
